/*!
    \page tutorial-map-and-sprites.html
    \title Tutorial 7: The Map, Sprites and Oblique Projection
    \previouspage {Tutorial 6: Connecting the Core to QML}
    \nextpage {Tutorial 8: Selecting Units and Giving Orders}

    \section1 The tile map

    Tiles are 16 by 16 units. Each creature item covers one tile:

    \quotefromfile qml/components/CreatureView.qml
    \skipto x: column * tileSize
    \printuntil height: tileSize

    A \c Behavior on \c x and \c y turns tile jumps into a smooth walk.

    \b {Why Repeater and not a tile map editor?} Felgo games often use Tiled maps. Our map
    is plain grass for now, so a \c Repeater is enough. A Tiled map is the way to go once
    the map gets terrain.

    \section1 Oblique projection

    Like in many classic 2D RPGs, height is drawn up and to the left. A sprite is 32 by 32, a tile 16 by 16,
    so we anchor the sprite's bottom right corner to the tile:

    \quotefromfile qml/components/CreatureView.qml
    \skipto readonly property real visualLeft
    \printuntil visualTop

    Objects further south or east are closer to the viewer and must be drawn on top:

    \quotefromfile qml/components/CreatureView.qml
    \skipto z: (x + y)
    \printline z:

    \section1 Sprite sheets

    Each creature has 12 frames: 4 directions, one idle and two walking frames each.
    Felgo reads TexturePacker sheets directly:

    \quotefromfile qml/components/CreatureView.qml
    \skipto sourceComponent: TexturePackerSpriteSequence {
    \printuntil frameNames: ["north_0.png"]
    \printline }

    Switch animations by name:

    \code
    creatureSprite.jumpTo("south_walk")
    \endcode

    \note Pack sheets with 1 pixel extrude and 2 pixels padding. Without it scaled pixel art
    shows thin lines at the frame edges.

    \section1 Screen densities

    Put each image in three sizes: normal, \c +hd (2x) and \c +hd2 (4x). Felgo picks one:

    \code
    assets/structures/base/base_1.png
    assets/structures/base/+hd/base_1.png
    assets/structures/base/+hd2/base_1.png
    \endcode

    Use the normal path in QML:

    \quotefromfile qml/components/BaseView.qml
    \skipto MultiResolutionImage {
    \printuntil }
    \printuntil }

    \section1 One grey image, many colours

    Bases are grey. A tiny shader multiplies them with the race colour:

    \quotefromfile shaders/tint.frag
    \skipto void main()
    \printuntil }

    The grey art comes from \c scripts/generate_placeholders.py.
*/
